oupled receptor that is involved in the signaling pathways activated by SCFA.", "GORDON ET AL:Gordon et al are researchers who studied the effects of microbial colonization on body weight and fat in germ-free mice.", "BÄCKHED ET AL:Bäckhed et al are researchers who contributed to the understanding of the relationship between gut microbiota and obesity.", "ACTINOBACTERIA:Actinobacteria is a phylum of bacteria that has been linked to obesity, showing increased representation in obese individuals.", "POLISACÁRIDOS INDIGERIBLES:Indigestible polysaccharides are substances that require microbiota to convert them into absorbable monosaccharides and short-chain fatty acids (SCFA), playing a role in energy extraction.", "M. SMITHII:M. smithii is a type of Archaea that converts hydrogen into methane, playing a role in the digestion of indigestible polysaccharides.", "RUMINCOCCUS:", "TURNBAUGH PJ:Turnbaugh PJ is a prominent researcher recognized for his significant contributions to the understanding of the human microbiome and its impact on health. He has conducted important experiments that demonstrate the causal relationship between microbiota and obesity, highlighting how microbial ecology is associated with this condition. Through his work, Turnbaugh PJ has advanced the field of microbiome research, particularly in relation to its implications for obesity and overall health.", "AKKERMANSIA:Akkermansia is a genus of bacteria that has been associated with gut health and obesity.", "RATONES:Experiments involving mice were conducted to study the effects of microbiota on obesity.", "GEMELOS HOMOCIGÓTICOS DISCORDANTES:Gemelos homocigóticos discordantes son gemelos que tienen diferentes características de obesidad, utilizados en estudios sobre microbiota y obesidad.", "RATONES GENÉTICAMENTE IDÉNTICOS:Rats that are genetically identical used in experiments to study the effects of diet on microbiota and obesity."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "temperature": 0.